  "initial_reflection": {
    "summary": "The run built a serviceable defensive Ironclad deck and survived Act 1, but Act 2 accumulated substantial HP loss while adding several redundant low-cost attacks. The final Collector fight became a critical-HP defense problem: the run reached 5/80 HP while an enemy was attacking, and the strongest defensive option was not played until after additional actions.",
    "lessons": [
      {
        "category": "CARD_REWARD",
        "title": "Stop accumulating redundant Reckless Charges",
        "situation": "When Reckless Charge is already present multiple times, especially in a small deck, evaluate whether another copy adds real value before taking it.",
        "lesson": "Prefer skipping or taking a more broadly useful damage/scaling card over repeatedly adding Reckless Charge unless the deck has a clear synergy that rewards the extra copies.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "Reckless Charge was bought in the Act 1 shop, then selected again at Act 2 Floor 18 over Clothesline+, and again at Act 2 Floor 24 over Carnage.",
          "The final 19-card deck contains three Reckless Charges, three Pommel Strikes, and three Shrug It Offs, showing substantial duplication in a relatively small deck."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The repeated selections concentrated the deck around similar low-cost attacks rather than adding distinct scaling or utility. Future reward decisions should treat the second or third copy as a higher bar, particularly when stronger alternatives are offered.",
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "category": "COMBAT",
        "title": "At critical HP, lead with the decisive defense",
        "situation": "When HP is near lethal range and an enemy has an attack intent, prioritize the most reliable large block before optional damage or smaller defensive plays.",
        "lesson": "At 5/80 HP with 0 block against an attacking enemy, play Impervious immediately when it is legal; do not begin the sequence with an optional attack unless the attack itself is necessary to prevent imminent lethal damage.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "On Turn 6 of the Collector fight, the player had 5/80 HP, 0 block, and the remaining Torch Head and Collector both had ATTACK intent.",
          "Impervious was a legal alternative at that exact state, but the actions began with Reckless Charge, then Shrug It Off, and Impervious was only played afterward."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The log does not provide exact incoming damage, so it cannot prove that a different order would have survived. However, with critical HP, 0 block, and an available high-certainty defense, spending the first action on damage created unnecessary risk and is the safer reusable priority.",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    ]
  },
  "review_decision": "HUMAN_TEACHING",
  "human_feedback": "Your combat planning and card selection showed some improvement this run. The deck remained relatively compact at 19 cards, you skipped several unnecessary rewards, and you correctly followed the previously taught strategy of targeting Centurion before Mystic. Continue retaining and applying lessons from all previous runs.\r\n\r\nHowever, your combat damage calculations are still inaccurate. Before approving a turn plan, calculate the actual incoming damage and the effective Block gained after accounting for player debuffs such as Frail. In the Collector fight, your verifier predicted 49 Block against 46 incoming damage on Turn 6, but the actual sequence generated only 36 Block, causing a fatal 10 HP loss when you had 5 HP remaining. A similar underestimation occurred on Turn 5. Do not calculate defense using the cards' printed Block values without accounting for current status effects. Your verifier must critically evaluate the full planned sequence instead of approving it based on incorrect calculations. Playing Impervious earlier in the same turn would not, by itself, have solved this problem.\r\n\r\nYour deck-building was more selective, but you still need to distinguish between strengthening an existing synergy and building sufficient damage scaling for longer boss fights. The Fire Breathing and Reckless Charge combination is a legitimate strategy because Reckless Charge generates Dazed cards, but additional copies should only be taken when they meaningfully improve the deck. You ended with three Reckless Charges and three Pommel Strikes, while your long-term scaling remained limited. At the Act 1 Floor 13 shop, you bought Reckless Charge instead of considering Demon Form, despite previously being taught its value. Evaluate whether a card solves an important weakness rather than repeatedly reinforcing an already functional part of the build.\r\n\r\nFinally, prioritize survival when approaching dangerous bosses. At the Act 2 Floor 32 campfire, you upgraded Bash while at 57/80 HP instead of resting before the Collector, despite having no potions remaining. Consider whether the additional HP would provide more value than a marginal upgrade, especially when your defense is inconsistent. Your objective is not simply to maximize card efficiency on individual turns, but to preserve enough HP and build sufficient damage scaling to survive and defeat the upcoming boss.",
  "finalized_at": "2026-10-19T03:08:20.449213+00:00"
}